))\\n\\n# Test orphan with wrong head\\norphan_wrong_head = {'body': {'entries_b64': [base64.b64encode(raw1).decode(), base64.b64encode(raw2).decode()]}}\\npred_wrong_head = dict(pred, head_sha256='0' * 64)\\nprint('Wrong head:', R._orphan_entries_problem(orphan_wrong_head, pred_wrong_head))\\n\\nprint('All tests passed!')\\n\\\"\"}"}}
{"type":"message_update","usage":{"input":6106,"output":1019,"cacheRead":17536,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":277,"totalTokens":24661,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0,"totalFromProvider":true}},"assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"All existing tests pass. Now let me verify the implementation by writing a quick test to ensure the new function works correctly. Let me also check if there are any edge cases I might have missed.\n\nActually, let me also think about the `_orphan_entries_problem` function more carefully. The task says:\n- `orphan[\"body\"][\"entries_b64\"]` is a list of base64-encoded canonical entry bytes (as produced by `R.canonical(entry)`).\n- Return `None` when the decoded entries are exactly the attested range: there are `to_seq - from_seq + 1` of them, the first chains from `pred[\"prev_sha256\"]` (its `prev` field), each next one chains from the previous one's sha256, and the sha256 of the last one's canonical bytes equals `pred[\"head_sha256\"]`.\n- Otherwise return a short problem string:\n  - one containing the word `range` when the count is wrong;\n  - one containing `chain` when an entry's `prev` doesn't link;\n  - one containing `head` when the last hash doesn't match `head_sha256`.\n\nMy implementation seems to match this. Let me verify by writing a quick test script to ensure the function works as expected."}}
